 Every column of a projection matrix is a projection vector, which means that the projection matrix projects every vector on the plane and onto the vector.  This means that the column vectors are in the plane, or perpendicular to the normal vector.  We do tests for all the columns.

The third row of the projection matrix shows that the dot product of $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ with $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 3 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}$ is $(3a+3b+5c),$ so $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ is normal to $(3,3,5),$ or normal to $(-3,-3,-5).$

The first and second rows also give us that $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ is normal to $\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix},$ which are skew and so also normal to each other.

So the normal vector is a common normal to $(-3,-3,-5),$ $(-1,3,3),$ and $(-1,-1,3),$ which means the normal vector is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 5 \\ -11 \end{pmatrix}},$ the cross product of $(-3,-3,-5)$ and $(-1,3,3)$, since they are not coplanar.

